|> Exercise
    !! work=ing
    handle=_more_closely_related_followup_

    For each of $\cos^2$, $\sin^2$, and $\sin\cdot\cos$:

    |> List
        marker=decimal

        Choose either $\cos(2x)$ or $\sin(2x)$ as a
        “most closely related starting point”, according
        to your sense of which of $\cos(2x)$ or $\sin(2x)$
        is more closely related to the given function.

        Conjecture a sequence of geometric transformations
        of the plane that takes either $\cos(2x)$ or $\sin(2x)$,
        depending on which you chose,
        !! as “most closely related”,
        onto the graph of the given function (be it $\cos\sin$,
        $\cos^2$, or $\sin^2$).

        Prove your conjecture by algebra.

    |> Solution

        For $\cos^2$ we take

        $$
        \cos(2x)
        $$

        as our starting point, since the graph of $\cos(2x)$
        seems more closely related to the graph of $\cos^2(x)$
        than $\sin(2x)$ is, by virtue of alignment of the bumps:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_more_closely_related_followup_sol1.svg

        Our conjecture, per appearance of the graphs, is that
        $\cos^2$ is the graph of $\cos(2x)$...

        __translated up by $1$, vertically
        scaled by $1/2$__

        |> ImageRight
            src=images/e5_more_closely_related_followup_cloud1.svg

        ...or is the graph of $\cos(2x)$...

        __vertically scaled by $1/2$,
        translated up by $1/2$__

        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/e5_more_closely_related_followup_cloud2.svg

        ...equivalently; for the proof,
        !! for the algebraic proof, we use the
        !! fact that 

        $$
        \cos(A + B) = \cos A\cos B - \sin A\sin B
        $$

        implies

        $$
        \cos(2x) = \cos^2 x - \sin^2 x
        $$

        by putting $A = B = x$, from which

        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        \phantom{=}&\,\,(\cos(2x) + 1)\cdot {1\over 2} \\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}(\cos^2 x - \sin^2 x + 1)\cdot {1\over 2} \\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}(\cos^2 x + \cos^2 x)\cdot {1\over 2} \\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}\cos^2 x
        \end{aligned}
        $$

        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/e5_more_closely_related_followup_cloud4.svg
            offset_y=1.3em

        which completes the proof, since
        $$(\cos(2x) + 1)\cdot {1\over 2}$$
        is $\cos(2x)$ translated up by $1$, scaled
        vertically by ${1\over 2}$.

        For $\sin^2$ we can similarly conjecture that
        $\sin^2$ is the graph of $\cos(2x)$

        __vertically scaled by $-1/2$,
        translated up by $1/2$__

        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/e5_more_closely_related_followup_cloud3.svg

        and, indeed,

        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        \phantom{=}&\,\,-{1\over 2}\cos(2x) + {1\over 2}\\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}-{1\over 2}(\cos^2 x - \sin^2 x) + {1\over 2}\\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}{1\over 2}\sin^2 x + {1\over 2}(1 - \cos^2 x)\\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}{1\over 2}\sin^2 x + {1\over 2}\sin^2 x\\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}\sin^2 x
        \end{aligned}
        $$

        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/e5_more_closely_related_followup_cloud5.svg
            offset_y=0.2em

        which completes a proof.

        For $\sin\cdot\cos$ it seems that $\sin(2x)$ is
        more closely related:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_more_closely_related_followup_sol3.svg

        In fact, it seems that $\sin\cdot\cos$ is just $y = \sin(2x)$

        __vertically scaled by $1/2$__

        which, indeed ($\times 2$), can be verified from the fact that

        $$
        \sin(A + B) = \cos A\sin B + \sin A\cos B
        $$

        implies 

        $$
        \sin(2x) = 2\sin x\cos x
        $$

        by setting $A = B = x$, which implies

        $$
        {1\over 2}\sin(2x) = \sin x\cos x
        $$

        as per the conjecture!

        |> SolutionNote

            Here is a “pretty” rearrangement of the formulas
            discovered above:

            |> Boxed
                $$
                \begin{gathered}
                \cos^2(x) = {1\over 2} + {1\over 2}\cos(2x) \\
                \up{2.1}\sin^2(x) = {1\over 2} - {1\over 2}\cos(2x) \\
                \up{2.1}\sin(x)\cos(x) = {1\over 2}\sin(2x)
                \end{gathered}
                $$


            Another pretty arrangement:

            |> Boxed
                $$
                \begin{gathered}
                \cos^2(x) = {1 + \cos(2x) \over 2}\\
                \up{2.1}\sin^2(x) = {1 - \cos(2x) \over 2} \\
                \up{2.1}\sin(x)\cos(x) = {\sin(2x) \over 2 }
                \end{gathered}
                $$
